---
import BaseLayout from '../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { seo } from '../../../config/seo';
import '../../../styles/smriti.css';

// Smriti privacy policy text (D038). This page is now the canonical copy.
---

<BaseLayout {...seo.smritiPrivacy} bodyClass="smriti-page" themeColor="#000000">
  <div class="smriti">
    <article class="sm-section sm-white sm-policy">
      <div class="container sm-policy-inner">
        <p class="sm-kicker">
          <a href="/products/smriti/">Smriti</a>
        </p>
        <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
        <p class="sm-note">Last updated: 7 November 2026</p>
        <p>
          Smriti is a personal activity journal for Android (package
          com.connectingdots.smriti). This policy explains what the app stores,
          what leaves your phone, and the choices you have.
        </p>

        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            There is no account, no sign-in, no analytics, no advertising and no
            Smriti server.
          </li>
          <li>
            Your journal is stored on your phone, and the app works without an
            internet connection. If you choose Google Drive backup, an encrypted
            copy goes to your own Drive.
          </li>
          <li>
            A few optional features use the internet, only at the moment you use
            them. They are listed below.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>What Smriti stores on your phone</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Your profile: name, optional photo, date of birth, gender —
            Personalises the app.
          </li>
          <li>
            Journal entries: date, time, activity, notes, place, people, tags —
            The core purpose of the app.
          </li>
          <li>
            People you add yourself (name, optional nickname and photo) — So you
            can record who you spent time with. Smriti does not read your
            phone's contacts.
          </li>
          <li>
            Saved places (name and optional map coordinates) — So you can record
            where an activity happened.
          </li>
          <li>
            Card cover images you choose — To decorate your monthly cards.
          </li>
          <li>
            App settings and a history of reminders shown — To run the app and
            avoid repeating reminders.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          This data is kept in the app's private storage. Your journal database
          and the photos you add (profile, people and card covers) are encrypted
          on the phone (AES-256) with a key held in the phone's secure Keystore;
          with App Lock on, that key only opens with your fingerprint. A copy of
          the key, locked with your backup passphrase, lets you get back in if
          the phone's Keystore is ever reset. Settings → Data &amp; Security →
          Delete Data, or uninstalling the app, removes it.
        </p>
        <p>
          If Android's device backup is turned on, it can copy only Smriti's
          latest encrypted auto-backup file. Your readable journal, photos and
          settings are excluded.
        </p>

        <h2>Permissions</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Location (only while the app is open) — When you save a place or log
            an activity, to fill in coordinates and suggest your nearest saved
            place. Never collected in the background.
          </li>
          <li>
            Microphone — Only while you use voice typing for notes. Smriti does
            not record or keep audio.
          </li>
          <li>
            Camera — Only when you choose "Take photo" for your profile or a
            person.
          </li>
          <li>
            Notifications — Reminders and "on this day" memories, created on
            your phone.
          </li>
          <li>
            Fingerprint / face / screen lock — Only if you turn on App Lock.
            Your phone checks it; Smriti never sees biometric data.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Photos are chosen with Android's system photo picker, so Smriti does
          not get access to your whole photo library.
        </p>

        <h2>What can leave your phone</h2>
        <p>
          Smriti never sends your journal anywhere on its own. These optional
          features need the internet and use other services:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>
            Voice typing uses your phone's speech-recognition service (on most
            Android phones this is Google). Depending on your phone it may run
            on the device or on the provider's servers, under that provider's
            privacy policy.
          </li>
          <li>
            Place names: when Smriti reads your location, it may ask your
            phone's geocoding service (usually Google) for the name of that
            spot.
          </li>
          <li>
            Maps (choosing a spot, or the small map in an entry) load map images
            from OpenStreetMap and the map code from unpkg.com. Like any
            website, these servers see your IP address and the map area shown,
            never your journal.
          </li>
          <li>
            Card cover from a web link: Smriti downloads that image once and
            keeps a copy on your phone. The website you link to sees your IP
            address.
          </li>
          <li>
            AI Insights export creates a plain-text file that you choose to
            share with another app. Nothing is sent unless you share it
            yourself.
          </li>
          <li>
            Backups are encrypted on your phone with your passphrase
            (AES-256-GCM) and saved only where you choose. Without the
            passphrase the file cannot be opened, and a lost passphrase cannot
            be recovered.
          </li>
          <li>
            Google Drive backup is off unless you connect your own Google
            account. Encrypted backup files then go straight from your phone to
            a folder in your Drive; there is no Smriti server in between. Smriti
            uses only the limited drive.file permission, so it can see only the
            files it creates there. Google can see the files' names, sizes and
            dates, and your Google account email is stored on your phone, but
            your journal can't be read without your passphrase. Disconnect at
            any time in Settings → Data &amp; Security → Auto-backup. Backups
            already in your Drive stay there until you delete them.
          </li>
          <li>
            Auto-backup key: so backups can run by themselves, a key made from
            your passphrase is kept in your phone's secure Keystore. Your
            passphrase itself is never saved.
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Smriti's use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
          Google API Services User Data Policy, including the Limited Use
          requirements. Data from your Google Drive is used only to create, read
          and delete Smriti's own backup files, is not shared with anyone, and
          is not used for advertising.
        </p>

        <h2>Children</h2>
        <p>
          Smriti is not directed at children under 13 and does not knowingly
          collect information from them.
        </p>

        <h2>Deleting your data</h2>
        <p>
          Use Settings → Data &amp; Security → Delete Data, or uninstall the
          app. There are no servers or accounts, so nothing else is held by us.
          Delete any .smriti backup files you saved yourself, including the
          "Smriti Backups - Do Not Delete" folder in your Google Drive if you
          used it.
        </p>

        <h2>Open source</h2>
        <p>
          Smriti is open source under the MIT License and is provided "as is",
          without warranty.
        </p>

        <h2>Changes</h2>
        <p>
          If this policy changes, the new version will be published at this
          address with a new "Last updated" date.
        </p>

        <h2>Contact</h2>
        <p>
          Email:
          <a href="mailto:akileshprimary@gmail.com">akileshprimary@gmail.com</a>
          — or use Share Feedback in Settings → About Application.
        </p>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
